Vulvodynia
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic severe burning vulvar pain >3 months, Exquisite pain upon light touch/penetration, Complete absence of visible dermatological disease, Negative high vaginal infectious swabs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-050
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, and agonizing burning vulvar pain causing profound distress, absolute inability to sit, and complete sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing acute, severe, unremitting burning pain in the vulvar region, resulting in an absolute inability to wear clothing, sit, or sleep. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a clinical examination, exclude an acute severe herpes simplex outbreak, acute Bartholin's abscess, or severe candidiasis, and provide immediate, potent topical analgesia prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is completely on fire down there, it feels like battery acid. I can't wear underwear, I can't sit, and the pain is driving me out of my mind."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute 48-hour severe flare of chronic, incredibly distressing pain localized strictly to the vulvar region, present intermittently for over a year. The pain is classically described as an intense burning, stinging, and severe sharp cutting sensation. The patient experiences both unprovoked, continuous burning and exquisite, agonizing pain upon the slightest distinct physical contact (provoked vestibulodynia), rendering them absolutely unable to wear tight clothing or engage in normal sexual intercourse (severe dyspareunia). The extreme, relentless pain has caused severe sleep deprivation tonight and significant associated secondary psychological distress. The patient explicitly denies unusual vaginal discharge, foul odor, or dysuria.
Functional Status: Standing or lying rigidly supine with legs apart, absolutely refusing to sit due to exquisite allodynia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound emotional distress, exhaustion, and severe anxiety regarding a lack of definitive structural diagnosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, tearful, wearing a loose robe, refusing to sit on the examination couch.
Physical Exam: Detailed, magnified visual inspection of the vulva and vestibule reveals completely normal, healthy-appearing pink mucosa. There is a complete, absolute absence of clinically relevant erythema, active infectious vaginitis, deep ulceration, vesicles, or primary dermatological disease (no lichen sclerosus). No palpable Bartholin's cysts or fluctuant abscesses. However, there is exquisitely painful, reproducible extreme tenderness upon very light touch to the vulvar vestibule utilizing a simple cotton swab (positive Q-tip test). Documented history of completely negative high vaginal swabs for active Candida or bacterial vaginosis, accompanied by historically negative biopsies.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty tubes of over-the-counter thrush treatments in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Chronic overuse of anti-fungal creams causing secondary chemical irritation, with absolutely zero relief of the primary pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vulvodynia - Status: Acute hyperalgesic neuropathic flare
Differentials:

Acute Vulvovaginal Candidiasis (Thrush) - Ruled out explicitly by the completely normal, pink mucosa lacking severe erythema or classic thick, white, cottage-cheese discharge, and historically negative swabs.

Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) Outbreak - Ruled out by the absolute absence of painful, grouped vesicles or shallow, punched-out mucosal ulcers on detailed inspection.

Bartholin's Abscess - Ruled out by the lack of a unilateral, hot, exquisitely tender, and fluctuant labial mass.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, agonizing, and continuous vulvar pain resulting in functional incapacitation (inability to sit or wear clothing) and acute psychological distress. The extreme burning sensation strongly mimicked an acute, severe infectious or dermatological emergency. An immediate, hands-on clinical and visual examination was completely mandatory to definitively rule out a severe HSV outbreak or acute abscess. By visually confirming entirely normal, healthy tissue architecture and identifying profound neuropathic allodynia via the Q-tip test, the physician definitively diagnosed a benign nerve-pain flare. This immediate clinical exclusion prevented a highly distressed and traumatic emergency department admission, allowing the safe deployment of targeted topical neuromodulators, strictly satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised immediate and absolute cessation of all over-the-counter anti-fungal and irritant creams. Prescribed a compounded topical Lidocaine 5% / Amitriptyline 2% ointment for direct, immediate localized neuropathic block.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of applying harsh chemical thrush treatments when the burning starts, aggressively apply a medical-grade cold gel pack wrapped in a soft cotton cloth directly to the vulva for 10 minutes. The intense, deep cold directly overloads the superficial nerve fibers, completely shutting off the severe burning signal (gate control theory) without damaging the fragile mucosa, immediately rewarding the patient with profound, non-chemical relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized pelvic floor physiotherapist and a dedicated women's health pain clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Perineal ice packs.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to discuss systemic neuropathic agents (e.g., oral Amitriptyline or Pregabalin).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate GP or ED presentation if developing a sudden, hot, swollen lump on one side of the labia, a high systemic fever, foul-smelling purulent discharge, or the sudden appearance of painful, fluid-filled blisters.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the completely normal visual inspection today; if visible sores, blisters, or new discharge appear, immediate re-evaluation with swabs is mandatory.
Clinical Course: Vulvodynia is a severe, chronic neuropathic pain condition. The tissue is physically healthy, but the tiny nerve endings in the skin are inflamed and misfiring, interpreting light touch as battery acid. It is exceptionally painful but completely physically benign and treatable with nerve-targeting therapies.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you notice a hot, swollen lump forming on one side, if painful fluid-filled blisters appear, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The visual exam we just performed proves there is absolutely zero yeast infection, bacteria, or skin disease present. Your pain is 100% real, but it is caused by the sensory nerves misfiring. All the over-the-counter thrush creams you have been using are actually chemically burning the hyper-sensitive nerves, making the pain worse.
Actionable Steps: Sleep completely without lower undergarments tonight. Physically removing all continuous fabric contact absolutely eliminates the mechanical friction that is constantly triggering the hyperactive nerve endings, which will directly break the severe allodynia cycle and allow you to finally sleep.

